4139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105829 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371193 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369629 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371152 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371159 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371235 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105830 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371242 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105834 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369636 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105834 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369643 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105834 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371214 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105834 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371221 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105834 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371228 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369594 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369601 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105835 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369608 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105866 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369337 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369865 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186105892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369872 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186106058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186102204 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186106058 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369845 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186106058 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186106058 1071 "![](images/f2/f299f0d25bfee9303a6485413d44f1a6ce7b863c79ccae91f4f8d5d604f2bac3.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-1}{4},\\, & 0\\le x<4, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,4)$\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n- $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186106058 1071 "![](images/f2/f299f0d25bfee9303a6485413d44f1a6ce7b863c79ccae91f4f8d5d604f2bac3.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n$f(x) = {\\frac{x - 1}{4},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x < 4, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f(x) \\ge 0$ for all $x ∈ [0, 4)$\n2. $\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{4}f(x)dx = 1$\n3. $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186106058 1074 "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. $f(x)$ is negative for some values of $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}\\frac{x-1}{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}x-1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x]}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\frac{16}{2}-4]-[\\frac{0}{2}-0]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(8-4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement I is false, $f(x)$ is not a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186106303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186103936 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186106303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370866 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186106303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186106303 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2.$\n- $v=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =0.$\n- If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2,$ then $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $\\lambda =6.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186106303 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $v = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ = 2$.\n2. $v = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is an eigenvector of $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue $λ = 0$.\n3. If $v$ is an eigenvector of a matrix $A$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $λ = 2$, then $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to $λ = 6$." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186106303 1074 "Remember that the eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ With that in mind, let's examine the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =1$ (but not $2$).\n\n- Statement II is true. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{v}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =0{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nSince $Av=0v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3v)=3Av=3(2v)=2(3v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince, $A(3v)=2(3v),$ and $3v\\ne 0,$ this implies that $3v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =2$ (but not $6$).\n\n**Note:** In general, any non-zero multiple of an eigenvector is again an eigenvector, and the corresponding eigenvalue is the same.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186106509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186105172 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186106509 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371146 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186106509 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186106509 1071 "A fair seven-sided die with sides labeled with numbers $1$ through $7$ is thrown once. If $X$ is the outcome of the die, what is the variance of $X$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186106509 1071 "A fair seven-sided die with sides labeled with numbers $1$ through $7$ is thrown once. If $X$ is the outcome of the die, what is the variance of $X?$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186106509 1074 "The variance of a discrete random variable $X$ with probability mass function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])^{2}\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute the probability mass function $f(x).$ Here, there are $7$ possible outcomes ($1,2,3,4,5,6,7$) and each outcome has the same probability, so the probability mass function is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ |\n\nNow, we need to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5)+6\\cdot f(6)+7\\cdot f(7) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{7})+2(\\frac{1}{7})+3(\\frac{1}{7})+4(\\frac{1}{7})+5(\\frac{1}{7})+6(\\frac{1}{7})+7(\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=4$ into the variance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]={\\sum }_{x\\in S}(x-4)^{2}\\cdot f(x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the variance as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =(1-4)^{2}\\cdot f(1)+(2-4)^{2}\\cdot f(2)+(3-4)^{2}\\cdot f(3)+(4-4)^{2}\\cdot f(4) \\\\  & +(5-4)^{2}\\cdot f(5)+(6-4)^{2}\\cdot f(6)+(7-4)^{2}\\cdot f(7) \\\\  & =9\\cdot f(1)+4\\cdot f(2)+1\\cdot f(3)+0\\cdot f(4)+1\\cdot f(5)+2\\cdot f(6)+9\\cdot f(7) \\\\  & =9(\\frac{1}{7})+4(\\frac{1}{7})+1(\\frac{1}{7})+0+1(\\frac{1}{7})+4(\\frac{1}{7})+9(\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186106514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186105202 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186106514 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371180 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186106514 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186106514 1071 "If $E[X] = 3$ and $E[X^{2}] = 11$, then what is $Var[X]$?" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186106514 1071 "If $\\text{E}[X]=3$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=11,$ then what is $\\text{Var}[X]?$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186106514 1074 "We can compute the variance of a discrete random variable $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =11-{3}^{2} \\\\  & =11-9 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186114980 1071 "Is the vector function $\\mathbf f(t) = -\\sin t \\,\\mathbf i + (t^2 + 1)\\,\\mathbf j + \\tan t\\,\\mathbf k$ continuous at $t = 0$?" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186114980 1071 "Is the vector function $\\mathbf f(t) = -\\sin t \\,\\mathbf i + (t^2 + 1)\\,\\mathbf j + \\tan t\\,\\mathbf k$ continuous at $t = 0?$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186114980 1074 "Let's compute the limit of $\\mathbf f (t)$ as $t$ approaches $0$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(t → 0)f(t) &= ⟨lim_(t → 0)(-\\sin t), lim_(t → 0)(t^{2} + 1), lim_(t → 0)\\tan t⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ - \\sin 0, 0^{2} + 1, \\tan 0⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨0, 1, 0⟩ \\\\\n&= f(0)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\rightarrow t_0} \\mathbf f (t) = \\mathbf f (t_0)\n$$\n\nand therefore $\\mathbf f (t)$ is continuous at $t=0$." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186114980 1074 "Let's compute the limit of $\\mathbf f (t)$ as $t$ approaches $0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 0}f(t) & =⟨{lim}_{t\\to 0}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}({t}^{2}+1),\\,{lim}_{t\\to 0}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,{0}^{2}+1,\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =f(0)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\displaystyle \\lim_{t \\rightarrow t_0} \\mathbf f (t) = \\mathbf f (t_0)$ and therefore $\\mathbf f (t)$ is continuous at $t=0.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186114981 1071 "Find $\\mathbf g(1)$ given that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $\\mathbf f(1) = \\left\\langle 1, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle$ and\n$\\lim_\\limits{t\\rightarrow 1} \\big[2\\mathbf f(t)-\\mathbf g(t) \\big] = \\left\\langle 2, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle$." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186114981 1071 "Find $\\mathbf g(1)$ given that $\\mathbf f(t)$ and $\\mathbf g(t)$ are two continuous vector-valued functions for which $\\mathbf f(1) = \\left\\langle 1, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle$ and \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{t\\rightarrow 1} \\big[ 2\\mathbf f(t)-\\mathbf g(t) \\big] = \\left\\langle 2, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186114981 1074 "Using the constant multiple rule and the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(t → 1)[2f(t) - g(t)] &= lim_(t → 1)2f(t) + lim_(t → 1)[- g(t)] \\\\\n&= 2lim_(t → 1)f(t) - lim_(t → 1)g(t) \\\\\n&= 2f(1) - g(1) \\\\\n&= 2⟨1, 1,-1⟩_(⏟)_(f(1)) - g(1) \\\\\n&= ⟨2, 2,-2⟩ - g(1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{t\\rightarrow 1} \\big[ 2\\mathbf f(t)-\\mathbf g(t) \\big] = \\left\\langle 2, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $\\mathbf{g} (1)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(t → 1)[2f(t) - g(t)] &= ⟨2, 1,-1⟩ \\\\\n⟨2, 2,-2⟩ - g(1) &= ⟨2, 1,-1⟩ \\\\\ng(1) &= ⟨2, 2,-2⟩ - ⟨2, 1,-1⟩ \\\\\ng(1) &= ⟨0, 1,-1⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186114981 1074 "Using the constant multiple rule and the addition rule for limits, and the fact that both functions are continuous, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)-g(t)] & ={lim}_{t\\to 1}2f(t)+{lim}_{t\\to 1}[-g(t)] \\\\  & =2{lim}_{t\\to 1}f(t)-{lim}_{t\\to 1}g(t) \\\\  & =2f(1)-g(1) \\\\  & =2{{⟨1,1,-1⟩}_{⏟}}_{f(1)}-g(1) \\\\  & =⟨2,2,-2⟩-g(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, we are told that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{t\\rightarrow 1} \\big[ 2\\mathbf f(t)-\\mathbf g(t) \\big] = \\left\\langle 2, 1, -1 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following equation which we can solve for $\\mathbf{g} (1)\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 1}[2f(t)-g(t)] & =⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ ⟨2,2,-2⟩-g(1) & =⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨2,2,-2⟩-⟨2,1,-1⟩ \\\\ g(1) & =⟨0,1,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186114982 1071 "Is the vector function $\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\left\\langle 2e^t, \\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{t-3}, \\: \\sqrt{t} \\right\\rangle$ differentiable with respect to $t$ on $t \\in [0, 2]$?" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186114982 1071 "Is the vector function $\\mathbf{f}(t) = \\left\\langle 2e^t, \\: \\dfra