}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=9 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the 