int\\limits_C P \\: \\text{d}x + Q \\: \\text{d}y = -\\oint\\limits_C y \\: \\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of $\\mathbf r_1(t)$ and $\\mathbf r_2(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=-8{t}^{3}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}y\\,dx-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}y\\,dx \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{3}\\cdot (-8{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}t-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =8{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{6}\\,\\text{d}t+0 \\\\  & =8[\\frac{{t}^{7}}{7}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\frac{2}{7} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133721 1071 "![](images/16/16026d4ac7deeb30c1abb3a066df23a06a544116463b364b0a1e9e0bac0608c8.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that\n\n$\\oint\\limits_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 10, \\qquad \\oint\\limits_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = -8, \\qquad \\oint\\limits_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 3$,\n\nwhere $C_1$ and $C_3$ are oriented clockwise, and $C_2$ is oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r$." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186133721 1071 "![](images/16/16026d4ac7deeb30c1abb3a066df23a06a544116463b364b0a1e9e0bac0608c8.png)\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C_1}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 10, \n\\qquad \n\\oint\\limits_{C_2}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = -8, \n\\qquad \n\\oint\\limits_{C_3}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r = 3,\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ and $C_3$ are oriented clockwise, and $C_2$ is oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133721 1074 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve $C_1$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_1$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves $C_2$ and $C_3$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_2$ and $C_3$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/11/11445c083440ee2fde1864d599a8bbd68146d522c61de11b3e90dc231e6eddf1.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer curve $C_1$ and the inner curve $C_2$ are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-10-(-8)+3 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133721 1074 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve $C_1$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_1$ counterclockwise), and\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves $C_2$ and $C_3$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse $C_2$ and $C_3$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/11/11445c083440ee2fde1864d599a8bbd68146d522c61de11b3e90dc231e6eddf1.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer curve $C_1$ and the inner curve $C_2$ are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(∂D)F \\cdot dr &= - ∮_(C_{1})F \\cdot dr - ∮_(C_{2})F \\cdot dr + ∮_(C_{3})F \\cdot dr \\\\\n&=-10 - (-8) + 3 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186133722 1071 "![](images/64/64ba92b021de2ba2f4fc98f1ce76fa0d16b3c8b7ed9ae45237cb8772b111c8b8.png)\n\n  \n\nCalculate \n$\\displaystyle\n\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} (2x^3y^2-x^5) \\,\\mathrm{d}x + (3x^4y+y^4) \\,\\mathrm{d}y,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133722 1071 "![](images/64/64ba92b021de2ba2f4fc98f1ce76fa0d16b3c8b7ed9ae45237cb8772b111c8b8.png)\n\nCalculate\n$\\displaystyle \\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} (2x^3y^2-x^5) \\,\\mathrm{d}x + (3x^4y+y^4) \\,\\mathrm{d}y$,\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186133722 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C$. If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y = \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem the same way as before.\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\nP(x,y)=2x^3y^2-x^5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nQ(x,y)= 3x^4y+y^4\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 4x^3y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 12x^3y\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∮_(∂D)(2x^{3}y^{2} - x^{5})dx + (3x^{4}y + y^{4})dy &= ∬_(D)(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} - \\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)12x^{3}y - 4x^{3}ydA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)8x^{3}ydA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as $\\Delta_1\\setminus\\Delta_2$, where\n\n- $\\Delta_1 = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 1, -1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n- $\\Delta_2 = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: -1 \\leq x \\leq 0, 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)8x^{3}ydA &= ∬_(Δ_{1})8x^{3}ydA - ∬_(Δ_{2})8x^{3}ydA \\\\\n&= \\int_{-1}^{2}\\int_{-2}^{1}8x^{3}ydxdy - \\int_{0}^{1}\\int_{-1}^{0}8x^{3}ydxdy \\\\\n&= \\int_{-2}^{1}4x^{3}dx\\int_{-1}^{2}2ydy - \\int_{-1}^{0}4x^{3}dx\\int_{0}^{1}2ydy \\\\\n&= \\left.x^{4}\\right|_{-2}^{1} \\cdot \\left.y^{2}\\right|_{-1}^{2} - \\left.x^{4}\\right|_{-1}^{0} \\cdot \\left.y^{2}\\right|_{0}^{1} \\\\\n&= (1 - 16) \\cdot (4 - 1) - (0 - 1) \\cdot (1 - 0) \\\\\n&= (-15) \\cdot 3 - (-1) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&=-44\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186133722 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x^3y^2-x^5$ and $Q(x,y)= 3x^4y+y^4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y} = 4x^3y, \\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = 12x^3y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(2{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-{x}^{5})\\,dx+(3{x}^{4}y+{y}^{4})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}12{x}^{3}y-4{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as $\\Delta_1\\setminus\\Delta_2,$ where\n\n- $\\Delta_1 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -2 \\leq x \\leq 1, -1 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- $\\Delta_2 = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq x \\leq 0, 0 \\leq y \\leq 1 \\big\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{1}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}8{x}^{3}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{1}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}2y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{0}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-2}^{1}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-{x}^{4}\\,{|}_{-1}^{0}\\cdot {y}^{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-16)\\cdot (4-1)-(0-1)\\cdot (1-0) \\\\  & =(-15)\\cdot 3-(-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133787 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371139 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133787 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371173 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133788 1038 #edb/ref 17592186370900 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369761 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133810 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369768 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133843 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371125 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369440 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369447 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369461 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133892 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369503 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133897 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369264 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133898 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369271 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133899 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369278 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133901 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369365 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133901 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369372 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133901 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369379 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133902 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369419 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133902 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369426 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133902 1038 #edb/ref 17592186370919 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133903 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369405 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133903 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369412 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133903 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369433 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133903 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369454 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369468 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369475 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369482 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369489 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133905 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369496 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369510 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369517 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369524 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186133906 1038 #edb/ref 17592186369531 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134088 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130654 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134088 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369258 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134088 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134088 1071 "![](images/f5/f531f2467284ef84e0af0758a8e8554f1e5f72ea08d30d0b80c83076fbe5d7a3.png)\n\nEvaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}(x + π\\cos πy)dx$, where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(1, 0)$ to the point $(2, 1)$, as shown above." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134088 1071 "![](images/f5/f531f2467284ef84e0af0758a8e8554f1e5f72ea08d30d0b80c83076fbe5d7a3.png)\n\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{C}(x+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y)\\,\\text{d}x,$ where $C$ is the path traversed along the line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(2,1),$ as shown above." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134088 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe curve $C$ is a line segment with endpoints $(1,0)$ and $(2,1).$ It can be parametrized as \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨(1+t(2-1)),\\,(0+t(1-0))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1+t,\\,t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=x+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t \\\\  & =1+t+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now evaluate the integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(1+t+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t)\\cdot 1\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+t+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[t+\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{{1}^{2}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi )-(0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130678 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134092 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369285 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134092 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134092 1071 "![](images/f8/f83043b44a8ad9181e66de57357397deb49c4b0e79b5e6e16330305a58391148.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to $\\displaystyle \\int_{C}xydy$, where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $4$, traversed from the point $(4, 0)$ to the point $(-4, 0)$, as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t) = ⟨4\\cos t, 4\\sin t⟩$.*" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134092 1071 "![](images/f8/f83043b44a8ad9181e66de57357397deb49c4b0e79b5e6e16330305a58391148.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}xy\\,\\text{d}y,$ where $C$ is the path along a circle of radius $4,$ traversed from the point $(4,0)$ to the point $(-4,0),$ as shown above?\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $r(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.$*" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134092 1074 "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe curve $C$ is a part-circle of radius $4$ centered at $O.$ It can be parameterized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t\\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }f(r(t))\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }16\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 4\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =64{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130714 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134098 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369325 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134098 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134098 1071 "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $P(x,y)=y$ and $Q(x,y)=-x$\n- $P(x,y)=\\frac{x}{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{2}$\n- $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134098 1071 "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n$∮_(C)Pdx + Qdy$\nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in $R^{2}$, which of the following could be the functions $P(x, y)$ and $Q(x, y)$?\n\n1. $P(x, y) = y$ and $Q(x, y) =-x$\n2. $P(x, y) = \\frac{x}{2} + \\cos y^{2}$ and $Q(x, y) = \\frac{y}{2}$\n3. $P(x, y) = 0$ and $Q(x, y) = x$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134098 1074 "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡{y}^{2}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130720 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134099 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369331 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134099 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134099 1071 "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the vector field $F(x,y)?$\n\n- $F(x,y)=⟨-y,\\,{x}^{2}+x⟩$\n- $F(x,y)=⟨-\\frac{y}{4}+x,\\,\\frac{3x}{4}⟩$\n- $F(x,y)=⟨-y,\\,0⟩$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134099 1071 "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n$∮_(C)F \\cdot dr$\nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in $R^{2}$, which of the following could be the vector field $F(x, y)$?\n\n1. $F(x, y) = ⟨ - y, x^{2} + x⟩$\n2. $F(x, y) = ⟨ - \\frac{y}{4} + x, \\frac{3x}{4}⟩$\n3. $F(x, y) = ⟨ - y, 0⟩$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134099 1074 "According to Green's theorem, if $F(x,y)=⟨P(x,y),\\,Q(x,y)⟩,$ where\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Vector field I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2x+1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x+2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field II gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\frac{1}{4}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\frac{3}{4},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\n- Vector field III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-1$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130762 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134106 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369386 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134106 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134106 1071 "![](images/52/52815b087f869769950d37ab6b95edb75accf030e742ed16b4679b6a41d7d638.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134106 1071 "![](images/52/52815b087f869769950d37ab6b95edb75accf030e742ed16b4679b6a41d7d638.png)\n\nCalculate $∮_(∂D)dx + xdy$, where $D$ is the annular region shown above." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134106 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=1$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(1-0)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\pi -\\pi  \\\\  & =3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186130768 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134107 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369399 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134107 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134107 1071 "![](images/6b/6bda5a10df8a1ec081db97e7aa52755796f1d50be5447e3e64f55986116ada6e.png)\n\nCalculate\n$∮_(∂D)xy^{2}dx + (x^{2}y + xy^{2})dy$,\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134107 1071 "![](images/6b/6bda5a10df8a1ec081db97e7aa52755796f1d50be5447e3e64f55986116ada6e.png)\n\nCalculate \n${∮}_{∂D}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134107 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2xy+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}x{y}^{2}\\,dx+({x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2xy+{y}^{2})-2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-4\\le x\\le 4,-3\\le y\\le 3}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-3}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{4}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-4}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-3}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =x\\,{|}_{-4}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-3}^{3}-x\\,{|}_{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}{|}_{-1}^{2} \\\\  & =(4-(-4))\\cdot (9-(-9))-(2-0)\\cdot (\\frac{8}{3}-(-\\frac{1}{3})) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 18-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =138.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186131761 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134292 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370907 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134292 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134292 1071 "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134292 1071 "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\n1. $U = {\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}$\n2. $V = {\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}$\n3. $W = {\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}, \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}}$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134292 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,\\,{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\\,{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=5-5=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- The set $V$ cannot be orthogonal since it contains  $3$ vectors from ${R}^{2}$ (in which we can have an orthogonal set with at most $2$ non-zero vectors). So, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]=4+0-4=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132217 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134368 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370926 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134368 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134368 1071 "A store collected data about its profits (in thousands of dollars) for the first five months since opening. This data is shown below.\n\n| Month $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $8.3$ | $5.1$ | $10.9$ | $10.7$ | $9.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the profit in the $6$th month, assuming that the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n\n$$\n({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}=\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{X}^{T}y=[\\begin{aligned}44.5 \\\\ 141.5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134368 1071 "A store collected data about its profits (in thousands of dollars) for the first five months since opening. This data is shown below.\n\n| Month $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Profit $(y)$ | $8.3$ | $5.1$ | $10.9$ | $10.7$ | $9.5$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y = β_{0} + β_{1}x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, what will be the profit in the $6$th month, assuming that the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n$(X^{T}X)^{-1} = \\frac{1}{10}\\begin{bmatrix}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, X^{T}y = \\begin{bmatrix}44.5 \\\\ 141.5\\end{bmatrix}$,\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $y$ is the observation vector.*\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134368 1074 "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{\\beta }$ to the system \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{X}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{\\beta }{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{\\beta }_{0} \\\\ {\\beta }_{1}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{y}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}8.3 \\\\ 5.1 \\\\ 10.9 \\\\ 10.7 \\\\ 9.5\\end{aligned}]}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\beta $ is the parameter vector, and $y$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by ${X}^{T},$ we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\n{X}^{T}\\,X\\overset{^}{\\beta }={X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X}^{T}X$ is invertible and \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\beta }=({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}y.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X}^{T}X)^{-1}$ and ${X}^{T}y$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\beta } & =({X}^{T}\\,X)^{-1}{X}^{T}\\,y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}11 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}44.5 \\\\ 141.5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6.5 \\\\ 0.8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line $y=6.5+0.8x.$\n\nFinally, to predict the profit in the $6$th month, we substitute $x=6$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =6.5+0.8(6) \\\\  & =11.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, according to the model, the profit in the $6$th month will be $$11\\,300.$\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=6$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134382 1068 #edb/ref 17592186132301 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134382 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370946 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134382 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134382 1071 "![](images/df/df940d9ea08ed1b326d4904933bd41c318112836d2d657abcb3c158aabbe7bb4.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve\n$C$ parametrized as $r(t) = 3\\sin ti + 2\\sin 2tj$\nfor $t ∈ [0, π]$." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186134382 1071 "![](images/df/df940d9ea08ed1b326d4904933bd41c318112836d2d657abcb3c158aabbe7bb4.png)\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region enclosed by the curve \n$C$ parametrized as $r(t)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j$ \nfor $t\\in [0,\\pi ].$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186134382 1074 "First, let $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0.$\n\nOur simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is negatively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0-(-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nNotice that we have to take the line integral with a minus sign since our path is negatively oriented. \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=-y$ and $Q(x,y)=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n-{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}y\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate our line integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}y\\,dx & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-12{\\int }_{1}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =12{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{u}^{2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4{{u}^{3}|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3}) \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186147440 1038 #edb/ref 17592186370992 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186147440 1038 #edb/ref 17592186371263 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186147441 1038 #edb/ref 17592186370985 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186148190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146864 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186148190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370973 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186148190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186148190 1071 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186148190 1071 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n$2x^{2}y^{″} - xy^{′} + y = 0, y(1) = 0, y^{′}(1) = 1, x > 0$." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186148190 1074 "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +1] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (2\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\lambda =1.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{1/2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{1}={c}_{1}\\sqrt{x}+{c}_{2}x.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2\\sqrt{x}}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 2 & ={c}_{1}+2{c}_{2} \\\\ 2 & ={c}_{1}-2{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{1} & =-2\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)=2x-2\\sqrt{x}.\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186148195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186146894 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186148195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370979 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186148195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186148195 1071 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+x{y}^{′}-4y=0,\\,y(1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=4,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186148195 1071 "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n$x^{2}y^{″} + xy^{′} - 4y = 0, y(1) = 0, y^{′}(1) = 4, x > 0$." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186148195 1074 "Note that the given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}-4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }-4{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -4] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+\\lambda -4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the roots are $\\lambda =\\pm 2.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-2}={c}_{1}{x}^{2}+\\frac{{c}_{2}}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nSubstituting $y(1)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0={c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-{c}_{1}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=1.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =2{c}_{1}x-2\\frac{{c}_{2}}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\ 2 & ={c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ 2 & ={c}_{1}-(-{c}_{1}) \\\\ {c}_{1} & =1\\,⇒\\,{c}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x)={x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186161814 1071 "![](images/78/78735529c93fa6286edc0fdc69d7398ec9cf758f7e46a0fb71702300aadbeb19.png)\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U?$\n![](images/70/703a545742438a81183008b90c0336ee51e9fc230d72ed44b02dbd76f0292f7f.png)" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186161814 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & ∗ \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & ∗\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times2$ matrix $A$, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U$?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186161814 1074 "Recall that since\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times2$ matrix $A$, the matrix $U$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that\n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1$.\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nhas both properties." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186161814 1074 "Recall that since $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $U$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186161815 1071 "![](images/ea/ea3e5c13d236a733350a1d23b8629cd481f9316e16ab7558873e305fe198eb98.png)\nConsider the matrices $A,$ $\\Sigma,$ and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186161815 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $A$, $\\Sigma$, and $V$ shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, what is the second column of $U$?" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186161815 1074 "Recall that if\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A$, and\n\n$$\n\\sigma_i \\neq 0\n$$\n\nthen the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf v_i$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V$.\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma$, we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\sigma_2=4$. Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu_{2} &= \\frac{1}{σ_{2}}Av_{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{4}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186161815 1074 "Recall that if $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and $\\sigma_i \\neq 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}_i = \\dfrac{1}{\\sigma_i} A\\mathbf{v}_i,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $\\mathbf v_i$ is the $i$th column of the matrix $V.$\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma,$ we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=9$ and $\\sigma_2=4.$ Therefore, the second column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}6 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{12}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186161816 1071 "![](images/bb/bb8991a8e02d7280b3d24587067ebf0d7e5f5c0518a9f6b1d022e65ed1d42780.png)\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|v_{11}|+|v_{21}|?$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186161816 1071 "$\\begin{bmatrix}17 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 17\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=U \\Sigma V^T$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, what is the value of $\\begin{vmatrix}v_{11} & + & v_{21}\\end{vmatrix}$?" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186161816 1074 "If\n\n$$\nA=U \\Sigma V^T\n$$\n\nis a singular value decomposition of $A$, the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A$, i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix $A^T \\! A$.\n\nFrom the matrix $\\Sigma$, we deduce that the singular values are $\\sigma_1=5$ and $\\sigma_2=3$. So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value $\\sigma_1=5$.\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of $A^T \\!A$ corresponding to\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 