t \\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =16\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =128.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186162371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186159398 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186162371 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369720 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186162371 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186162371 1071 "A $2 \\cdot 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x \\mid ‖x‖ = 1}$ from $R^{3}$ onto an ellipse in $R^{2}$. Given that the singular values of $A$ are $σ_{1} = 3$, $σ_{2} = 2\\sqrt{2}$, and $σ_{3} = 0$, find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186162371 1071 "A $2\\times 3$ matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto an ellipse in ${R}^{2}.$ Given that the singular values of $A$ are ${\\sigma }_{1}=3,$ ${\\sigma }_{2}=2\\sqrt{2},$ and ${\\sigma }_{3}=0,$ find the length of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186162371 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit sphere ${x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ from ${R}^{3}$ onto the ellipse ${Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=3,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={3}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax{‖}^{2}}={3}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}{‖Ax‖}=3.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=3.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186162667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161264 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186162667 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371277 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186162667 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186162667 1071 "$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\,∗\\, \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\,∗\\,\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U?$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186162667 1071 "$U = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & ∗ \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & ∗\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix $U$ shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2 \\cdot 2$ matrix $A$, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $U$?" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186162667 1074 "Recall that since $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $2\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ the matrix $U$ must be a $2\\times 2$ orthogonal matrix. This means that \n\n- the first column must be orthogonal to the second column, and\n\n- the norm of the second column must be equal to $1.$\n\nAmong the given options, only the vector $[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]$ has both properties." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186162673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186161300 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186162673 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371283 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186162673 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186162673 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186162673 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, Σ = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrices $A$ and $Σ$ shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V$?" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186162673 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: From the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=4$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=2.$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the right singular vectors of $A$ (i.e., the unit eigenvectors of ${A}^{T}\\,A$):\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=16,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-16I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]-16[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-16I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}={\\sigma }_{2}^{2}=4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 10\\end{aligned}]-4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-4I)v=0$ gives a unit eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- **Step 4.** Find the left singular vectors of $A$: Since we know the singular values and right singular vectors, we can compute the columns of $U$ as follows:\n\n- First, we compute ${u}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2} \\\\ -2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute ${u}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{2}}A{v}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we construct the matrices $U$ and $V:$\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186166560 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-4x+7xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=9y-5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186166560 1071 "${x^{′} =-4x + 7xy; y^{′} = 9y - 5xy$\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186166560 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)\n$$\n\nare the points for which\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{-4x + 7xy = 0; 9y - 5xy = 0\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-4x + 7xy &= 0 \\\\\nx(-4 + 7y) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac{4}{7}\n$$\n\nWe consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0$, substituting it into the second equation, we have\n$9y - 5(0)y = 0⇒y = 0$.\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0)$.\n- If $y=\\dfrac{4}{7}$, substituting it into the second equation, we have\n$9y - 5xy|= 0; y(9 - 5x)|= 0; \\frac{4}{7}(9 - 5x)|= 0; 9 - 5x|= 0; x|= \\frac{9}{5}$.\nThus, another equilibrium is $\\left(\\dfrac{9}{5}, \\dfrac{4}{7}\\right)$.\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{9}{5}, \\dfrac{4}{7} \\right)\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186166560 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4x+7xy=0 \\\\ 9y-5xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+7xy & =0 \\\\ x(-4+7y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\dfrac{4}{7}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-5(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\dfrac{4}{7},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9y-5xy & =0 \\\\ y(9-5x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4}{7}(9-5x) & =0 \\\\ 9-5x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $\\left(\\dfrac{9}{5}, \\dfrac{4}{7}\\right).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$\\left( \\dfrac{9}{5}, \\dfrac{4}{7} \\right).$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186166561 1071 "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-0.4x+0.4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=y-4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.25, \\, 1]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186166561 1071 "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey.\n${x^{′} =-0.4x + 0.4xy; y^{′} = y - 4xy$\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.25, \\, 1]^T$, and classify this equilibrium point." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186166561 1074 "Let's denote\n\n$$\nf_1(x,y)=-0.4x+0.4xy\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf_2(x,y)=y-4xy\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(-0.4x + 0.4xy) =-0.4 + 0.4y, & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-0.4x + 0.4xy) = 0.4x \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(y - 4xy) =-4y, & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y - 4xy) = 1 - 4x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-0.4 + 0.4y & 0.4x \\\\ -4y & 1 - 4x\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $\\begin{bmatrix}0.25 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-0.4 + 0.4(1) & 0.4(0.25) \\\\ -4(1) & 1 - 4(0.25)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}- λ & 0.1 \\\\ -4 & - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(- λ)(- λ) - (-4) \\cdot 0.1 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 0.4 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm 0.2\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{i}\n$$\n\nSince the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is a stable center.\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model (as in this problem), it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues.\n\n**Watch out!**\nIn contrast, for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone is inconclusive (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source)." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186166561 1074 "Let's denote $f_1(x,y)=-0.4x+0.4xy$ and $f_2(x,y)=y-4xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-0.4x+0.4xy)=-0.4+0.4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-0.4x+0.4xy)=0.4x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-4xy)=-4y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-4xy)=1-4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](images/e5/e526dd9bda292c2a25920b8248820f3ed88c0bc4c4a55e3ba3f2db925af486d8.png)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/cc/cc6e0f2742f419fc8f2c09bf69c97f72d7e7f1ca0d98c3845996e216849f4316.png) to get\n\n![](images/bc/bcc787d9cceae4a7280c6af84c0d7bf95c15dfc90cf9e3bdedf2a52362c1b225.png)\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & 0.1 \\\\ -4 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-(-4)\\cdot 0.1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.4 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm 0.2\\sqrt{10}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is a stable center.\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model (as in this problem), it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues. \n\n**Watch out!** \nIn contrast, for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone is inconclusive (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source)." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186166562 1071 "Consider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-10x+5xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=21y-7xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x=x(t), y=y(t),$ and $a,b,c,$ and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nDescribe the trajectory and the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n![](images/86/86002a25d2c60e07c34976541a1c050e606d20e4ffa7928a44f940bf5fa736e3.png)" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186166562 1071 "Consider the phase portrait above showing a solution of a Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model\n${x^{′} =-10x + 5xy; y^{′} = 21y - 7xy$\nwhere $x=x(t), y=y(t)$, and $a,b,c$, and $d$ are positive constants.\n\nDescribe the trajectory and the dynamics of the system from point $A$ to point $B$ on the solution.\n\n![](images/86/86002a25d2c60e07c34976541a1c050e606d20e4ffa7928a44f940bf5fa736e3.png)" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186166562 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-ax+bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=cy-dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey.\n\nWith that in mind, let's describe the system:\n\n- The trajectory circles around the nonzero equilibrium.\n\n- From $A$ to $B,$ the number of prey decreases and the number of predators increases." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186166562 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n{x^{′} =-ax + bxy; y^{′} = cy - dxy\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the predators and $y=y(t)$ represents the prey.\n\nWith that in mind, let's describe the system:\n\n- The trajectory circles around the nonzero equilibrium.\n- From $A$ to $B$, the number of prey decreases and the number of predators increases." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186166563 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=-8x+2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=4y-xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t).$ Provide an interpretation of each equation in the model." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186166563 1071 "${x^{′} =-8x + 2xy; y^{′} = 4y - xy$\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above for the functions $x(t)$ and $y(t)$. Provide an interpretation of each equation in the model." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186166563 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants. \n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a rate of change.\n\nFor the $x'$ equation, we have\n\n$$\nx' = -8x + 2xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x$ must represent the predator. Indeed,\n\n- it decays exponentially in the absence of prey (the $-8x$ term), and\n\n- increases when interacting with prey (the $2xy$ term).\n\nFor the $y'$ equation, we have\n\n$$\ny' = 4y - xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$ must represent the prey. Indeed, \n\n- it grows exponentially in the absence of predators (the $4y$ term), and\n\n- decreases when interacting with predators (the $-xy$ term)." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186166563 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model is given by\n\n$$\n{x^{′} = ax - bxy; y^{′} =-cy + dxy\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d$ are positive real constants.\n\nFirst, recall that a derivative represents a rate of change.\n\nFor the $x'$ equation, we have\n\n$$\nx' = -8x + 2xy\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x$ must represent the predator. Indeed,\n\n- it decays exponentially in the absence of prey (the $-8x$ term), and\n- increases when interacting with prey (the $2xy$ term).\n\nFor the $y'$ equation, we have\n\n$$\ny' = 4y - xy\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y$ must represent the prey. Indeed,\n\n- it grows exponentially in the absence of predators (the $4y$ term), and\n- decreases when interacting with predators (the $-xy$ term)." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186166564 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=5x(1-0.2x)-4xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+3xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the equilibria of the model." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186166564 1071 "${x^{′} = 5x(1 - 0.2x) - 4xy; y^{′} =-6y + 3xy$\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the equilibria of the model." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186166564 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)\n$$\n\nare the points for which\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{5x(1 - 0.2x) - 4xy = 0; - 6y + 3xy = 0\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6y + 3xy &= 0 \\\\\ny(-6 + 3x) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2$. We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0$, substituting it into the first equation, we have\n$5x(1 - 0.2x) - 4x(0)|= 0; 5x(0.2x - 1)|= 0; x|= 0, 5$.\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(5,0)$.\n- If $x=2$, substituting it into the first equation, we have\n$5(2)(1 - 0.2(2)) - (4)(2)y|= 0; 10(1 - 0.4) - 8y|= 0; 10(0.6) - 8y|= 0; 6 - 8y|= 0; y|= 0.75$.\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2, 0.75)$.\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n$(0,0), \\qquad (5, 0), \\qquad (2, 0.75)$." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186166564 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system $\\mathbf{x}'(t) = \\mathbf{f}(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=\\mathbf{0}.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}5x(1-0.2x)-4xy=0 \\\\ -6y+3xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6y+3xy & =0 \\\\ y(-6+3x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=2.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x(1-0.2x)-4x(0) & =0 \\\\ 5x(0.2x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(5,0).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(2)(1-0.2(2))-(4)(2)y & =0 \\\\ 10(1-0.4)-8y & =0 \\\\ 10(0.6)-8y & =0 \\\\ 6-8y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(2, 0.75).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n(5, 0), \\qquad\n(2, 0.75).\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186166565 1071 "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity below, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=5x(1-x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.5, \\, 2.5]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186166565 1071 "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity below, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n${x^{′} = 5x(1 - x) - xy; y^{′} =-y + 2xy$\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.5, \\, 2.5]^T$, and classify this equilibrium point." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186166565 1074 "Let's denote\n\n$$\nf_1(x,y)=5x(1-x)-xy\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf_2(x,y)=-y+2xy\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x(1 - x) - xy) = 5 - 10x - y, & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x(1 - x) - xy) =-x \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y + 2xy) = 2y, & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y + 2xy) =-1 + 2x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}5 - 10x - y & -x \\\\ 2y & -1 + 2x\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 2.5\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}5 - 10(0.5) - 2.5 & -0.5 \\\\ 2(2.5) & -1 + 2(0.5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{vmatrix}-2.5 - λ & -0.5 \\\\ 5 & - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(-2.5 - λ)(- λ) - 5(-0.5) &= 0 \\\\\nλ(2.5 + λ) + 2.5 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + 2.5λ + 2.5 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\lambda_{1,2} = -1.25 \\pm 0.25\\sqrt{15}\\,\\text{i}\n$$\n\nSince the eigenvalues are complex with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an asymptotically stable spiral sink." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186166565 1074 "Let's denote $f_1(x,y)=5x(1-x)-xy$ and $f_2(x,y)=-y+2xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(5x(1-x)-xy)=5-10x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5x(1-x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y+2xy)=2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y+2xy)=-1+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n![](images/b0/b046e51c9a1b68cc22e1bd46f532a8d8d70452e5549937aaa8d872e60c63eb20.png)\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ![](images/e9/e91d26ba55bafc85ce58c65482858d3f2094136b9620fe5d6a30b514ffe59b87.png) to get\n\n![](images/dc/dcdb4709be5d408bf054292b36e9bd898db3047b4c66230fc9249fc57e229b2b.png)\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-2.5-\\lambda  & -0.5 \\\\ 5 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-2.5-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-5(-0.5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (2.5+\\lambda )+2.5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2.5\\lambda +2.5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues $\\lambda_{1,2} = -1.25 \\pm 0.25\\sqrt{15}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are complex with negative real parts, our equilibrium is an asymptotically stable spiral sink." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186166566 1071 "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity below, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=4x(1-2x)-3xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-5y+4xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.5, \\, 0]^T,$ and classify this equilibrium point." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186166566 1071 "Consider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity below, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n${x^{′} = 4x(1 - 2x) - 3xy; y^{′} =-5y + 4xy$\nEvaluate the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium point $\\mathbf{x^\\ast}=[0.5, \\, 0]^T$, and classify this equilibrium point." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186166566 1074 "Let's denote\n\n$$\nf_1(x,y)=4x(1-2x)-3xy\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf_2(x,y)=-5y+4xy\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂x} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x(1 - 2x) - 3xy) = 4 - 16x - 3y, & \\frac{∂f_{1}}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x(1 - 2x) - 3xy) =-3x \\\\ \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(-5y + 4xy) = 4y, & \\frac{∂f_{2}}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-5y + 4xy) =-5 + 4x\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 - 16x - 3y & -3x \\\\ 4y & -5 + 4x\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium $\\begin{bmatrix}0.5 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 - 16(0.5) - 3(0) & -3(0.5) \\\\ 4(0) & -5 + 4(0.5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1 = -4\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=-3\n$$\n\nSince the eigenvalues are real and both negative, our equilibrium is an asymptotically stable sink." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186166566 1074 "