y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.25x)-xy=0 \\\\ -6y+2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6y+2xy & =0 \\\\ y(-6+2x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=3.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.25x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.25x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(4,0).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)(1-0.25(3))-(3)y & =0 \\\\ 1.5-3y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(3,0.5).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,0),\\,(3,0.5).\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164700 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186167261 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370160 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167261 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167261 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.25x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-6y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.\n\nThe maximum carrying capacity is {{field-1}}." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167261 1071 "${x^{′} = 2x(1 - 0.25x) - xy; y^{′} =-6y + 2xy$\nConsider the modified Lotka-Volterra model, where $x = x(t)$ represents the prey and $y = y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators.The maximum carrying capacity is {{field-1}}." 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186167261 1074 "The Lotka-Volterra predator-prey model with carrying capacity is given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,c,d,h$ are positive real constants. Here, $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators.\n\nIn our case, the equation describing the dynamics of the prey is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.25x)-xy\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the $2x(1-0.25x)$ term defines the growth, and\n\n- the $-xy$ term defines the decay due to interaction with predators.\n\nIn the case of the complete absence of predators, we have $y=0,$ and this equation becomes\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}=2x(1-0.25x).\n$$\n\nGeneral solutions to this equation are logistic functions with two horizontal asymptotes defined by the equilibrium solutions, which can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.25x)=0\\,⇒\\,x=0,\\,4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equilibria are $x=0$ and $x=4.$ The nonzero equilibrium gives the maximum capacity.\n\nTherefore, the maximum carrying capacity for the prey in the case of the complete absence of predators is $4.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164701 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186167262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186164702 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186167262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370183 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167262 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370185 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167262 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167262 1071 "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=3x-2xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-4y+5xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.\n\n$(${{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}$)$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167262 1071 "${x^{′} = 3x - 2xy; y^{′} =-4y + 5xy$\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model above, where $x = x(t)$ represents the prey and $y = y(t)$ represents the predators. Find the nonzero equilibrium of the model.$($ {{field-1}}\n$$,\n{{field-2}} $)$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186167262 1074 "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{′}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-2xy=0 \\\\ -4y+5xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2xy & =0 \\\\ x(3-2y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x=0$ or $y=\\frac{3}{2}.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $x=0,$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+5(0)y=0\\,⇒\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, one equilibrium is $(0,0).$\n\n- If $y=\\frac{3}{2},$ substituting it into the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+5xy & =0 \\\\ y(-4+5x) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2}(-4+5x) & =0 \\\\ -4+5x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nTherefore, the nonzero equilibrium of the model is\n$(\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{2}).$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165002 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186167303 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370591 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167303 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167303 1071 "$$\n{y}^{‴}-2{y}^{″}-5{y}^{′}+6y=20x{e}^{3x},\\,{{y}_{1}={e}^{-2x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{3x}}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={u}_{1}(x){e}^{-2x}+{u}_{2}(x){e}^{x}+{u}_{3}(x){e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{3}(x)?$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167303 1071 "$y^{‴} - 2y^{″} - 5y^{′} + 6y = 20xe^{3x}, {y_{1} = e^{-2x}, y_{2} = e^{x}, y_{3} = e^{3x}}$\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above.\nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution of the equation can be written in the form\n$y_{p}(x) = u_{1}(x)e^{-2x} + u_{2}(x)e^{x} + u_{3}(x)e^{3x}$.\nWhat is the function $u_{3}(x)$?" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186167303 1074 "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=20x{e}^{3x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-2x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x} & {e}^{3x} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x} & 3{e}^{3x} \\\\ 4{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x} & 9{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({e}^{-2x}\\cdot {e}^{x}\\cdot {e}^{3x})|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 9\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[1(9-3)-1(-18-12)+1(-2-4)] \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(6+30-6) \\\\  & =30{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{3},$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{3}(x) & ={W}_{3}({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2x} & {e}^{x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}-(-2{e}^{-x}) \\\\  & =3{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3}(x) & =(-1)^{3-3}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{3}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{(20x{e}^{3x})(3{e}^{-x})}{30{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int 2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186166022 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186167474 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371401 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167474 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167474 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V?$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186167474 1071 "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nConsider the matrix shown above. Given that $A = UΣV^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the matrices $U$ and $V$?" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186167474 1074 "Let's find the singular value decomposition of $A$ using the usual algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** Compute ${A}^{T}\\,A:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the singular values: Notice that since ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is diagonal, the singular values of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\sigma }_{1}={\\sigma }_{2}=1.$\n\n- **Ste