)\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $e^{At} = \\begin{bmatrix}\\cosh (3t) & \\sinh (3t) \\\\ \\sinh (3t) & \\cosh (3t)\\end{bmatrix}$, where $A = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$.*\n\n| $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} =$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n|  | $\\sinh (3t)$ |  |  |" 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186229684 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t-s)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t-s)) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t-s)) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3(t-s))\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(3t)-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sinh}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186229689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228285 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186229689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228290 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186229689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228295 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186229689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228300 13194139533449 17592186045585 false] [17592186229689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370356 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186229689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370361 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186229689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370366 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186229689 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370371 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186229689 1070 #edb/ref 