that the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+4x-3$ has a root in the interval $(\\frac{1}{2},1),$ find the sum of the **distinct** real roots of $f(x).$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369174 1072 #uuid "860da3c6-1fe3-51f9-bc82-a47ff61bc088" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369174 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-3$ and the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-3}{\\text{factor of }4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{4},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 3\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ is $\\frac{3}{4}.$\n\nUsing the synthetic division, we get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $4$ | $-3$ | $4$ | $-3$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $3$ | $0$ | $3$ |\n|  | $4$ | $0$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{3}{4})(4{x}^{2}+4) \\\\  & =4(x-\\frac{3}{4})({x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =(4x-3)({x}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{2}+1\\ne 0$ for any real number $x,$ the only root of $f(x)$ is $x=\\frac{3}{4}.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369175 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369176 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369175 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369177 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369175 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369178 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369175 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369179 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369175 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369180 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [175921863691