449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369181 1072 #uuid "44fa5882-977b-5064-93c3-ee10e2d95b5e" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369181 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-3$ and the leading coefficient is $18.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-3}{\\text{factor of }18}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{18},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{9},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{6},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only root in the interval $(1,3)$ is $\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nUsing the synthetic division, we get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $18$ | $-15$ | $-16$ | $-3$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $27$ | $18$ | $3$ |\n|  | $18$ | $12$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{3}{2})(18{x}^{2}+12x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{3}{2})(18{x}^{2}+12x+2) \\\\  & =2(x-\\frac{3}{2})(9{x}^{2}+6x+1) \\\\  & =(2x-3)(3x+1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{3}{2}.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{3}{2}=\\frac{7}{6}.\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369182 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369183 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369182 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369184 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369182 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369185 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369182 1075 #edb/re