}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nSo, the curve $C$ is an ellipse centered at $O$ with horizontal and vertical semiaxes of $3\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2},$ respectively. Therefore, it can be parametrized as \n\n$$\nr(t)=⟨3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)j \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{18{sin}^{2}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+2({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=2-{y}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =2-(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2} \\\\  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡t \\\\  & =2(1-{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}(2-{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }2{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡t\\sqrt{16{sin}^{2}⁡t+2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369224 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369225 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369224 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369226 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369224 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369227 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369224 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369228 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369224 1075 #edb/ref 1