x)=5{x}^{3}+3x$ on the interval $[0,2].$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369991 1072 #uuid "7a032582-bf84-5821-854a-1d2629221d87" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369991 1074 "The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[0,2]$ and differentiable on $(0,2),$ hence we can apply the mean value theorem. \n\nSo, the mean value theorem states that there exists a $c\\in (0,2)$ such that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=\\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}.\n$$\n\nPlugging in $a=0$ and $b=2$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(c) & =\\frac{f(2)-f(0)}{2-(0)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(5\\cdot {2}^{3}+3\\cdot 2)-(5\\cdot {0}^{3}+3\\cdot 0)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{46-0}{2} \\\\  & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{x}^{3}+3x)=15{x}^{2}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(c)=15{c}^{2}+3.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15{c}^{2}+3 & =23 \\\\ 15{c}^{2} & =20 \\\\ {c}^{2} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ c & =\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $c\\in (0,2)$, we discard the negative value, therefore $c=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369992 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369993 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369992 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369994 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369992 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369995 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369992 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369996 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369992 1075 #edb/ref 17592186369997 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369992 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369992 1077 "selection" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369992 1079 #edb/ref 17592186369996 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369992 1080 #uuid "8e967df0-a873-53b9-9657-5a0a616c553a" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369993 1081 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369993 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369993 1084 #uuid "6c23005f-935e-5aed-aef9-971bdc776860" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369994 1081 "\\pm \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369994 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369994 1084 #uuid "3ce5d6a3-8a5d-598b-afee-6b7ef24b583f" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369995 1081 "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369995 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369995 1084 #uuid "638f1922-9f3c-56eb-93ab-cf57fbad2ea5" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369996 1081 "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369996 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369996 1084 #uuid "c42f4785-cd33-5e0a-98f3-f798339d2f2d" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369997 1081 "\\sqrt{3}\\,,\\sqrt{\\frac{4}{3}}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369997 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369997 1084 #uuid "ad1c4b7a-cd6c-5af0-a758-652b8e6ec472" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369998 1068 #edb/ref 17592186369999 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369998 1069 "q-2553" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369998 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369998 1071 "![](images/2e/2ef2330edb3a3822e6edd8bd443238d027e45e1a8b12ceb8b6be53f9fa68fbd7.png)\n\nA large latex balloon is inflated with helium so that its radius increases at a rate of $3\\,\\text{in/s}.$ How fast is the volume of the balloon increasing at the moment when the radius is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{in}?$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369998 1072 #uuid "faba5f32-b1bb-5241-b92d-96c4c3ee07fe" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369998 1074 "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,\\text{in/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{in}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3$ and $r=\\frac{3}{2},$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =3\\cdot 4\\pi {(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =27\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the balloon is increasing at a rate of $27\\pi \\,{\\text{in}}^{3}\\text{/s}$\nwhen the radius is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{in}.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369999 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370000 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369999 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370001 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369999 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370002 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369999 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370003 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369999 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370004 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369999 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369999 1077 "selection" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369999 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370000 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186369999 1080 #uuid "2b3bdad1-637c-571d-bc3b-a573f05e3abb" 13194139533449 17592