 of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=6$ and the vertical radius has length $b=2.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370006 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370007 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370006 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370006 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370006 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370007 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370006 1080 #uuid "2effdd59-bc2f-5ad0-a07a-23dc11d9d6e0" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370007 1081 "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370007 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370007 1084 #uuid "0a085c46-201d-5ed3-be42-2030b699b7e5" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370008 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370009 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370008 1069 "q-279127" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370008 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370008 1071 "![](images/92/92752dfc375c895c72d3a053a9abfd64eb64ecf9660e24b0c731e7ae9616ae7b.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370008 1072 #uuid "d8eb6498-fe7d-54d8-8156-e7257b7cf3ba" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370008 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{15}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{5}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{(\\frac{15}{2})}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{225}{4})}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\frac{25}{4})} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{225}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370009 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370010 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370009 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370009 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370009 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370010 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370009 1080 #uuid "24b13a75-2938-57e9-b343-79d1cfb33366" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370010 1081 "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{225}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{25}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370010 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370010 1084 #uuid "f7341349-5722-538a-a46b-c46b327387d6" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370011 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370012 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370011 1069 "q-279141" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370011 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370011 1071 "![](images/24/244cfd754107b056748bd6049c2996f9c0a0ae990f2033005b2f96e5abf1c0ea.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370011 1072 #uuid "ac9d8686-4f69-5072-a6be-b7169a3cf7dd" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370011 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=4$ and the vertical radius has length $b=9.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{9}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370012 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370013 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370012 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370012 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370012 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370013 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370012 1080 #uuid "f45ec21e-afa6-5535-91ea-60e2824f8700" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370013 1081 "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{81}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370013 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370013 1084 #uuid "58f12915-dd40-5ab5-9c8d-d5b8239ddb65" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370014 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370015 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370014 1069 "q-279143" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370014 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370014 1071 "![](images/68/682eaeaa8b8ef428ab181953ab577e1fe91009996b83f0799a7684d8b9efb21d.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370014 1072 #uuid "d4d8222d-c914-54e1-8111-88a395566578" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370014 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=4$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{15}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(\\frac{15}{2})}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\frac{225}{4})} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{225} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370015 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370016 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370015 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370015 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370015 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370016 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370015 1080 #uuid "d5e92adb-417b-51d5-9a32-861203d3639b" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370016 1081 "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{225}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370016 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370016 1084 #uuid "a34b4a4f-ec93-5b79-9836-24c30f1a306c" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370017 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370018 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370017 1069 "q-279145" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370017 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370017 1071 "![](images/4e/4e5123d9581d8b9ee1cc32358519c505c47f6e976a2fa28ff1939c769bebcdc9.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370017 1072 #uuid "1c883bff-1508-545d-b07f-f784ee6e3f19" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370017 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{17}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(\\frac{17}{2})}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\frac{289}{4})} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{289} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370018 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370019 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370018 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370018 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370018 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370019 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370018 1080 #uuid "a21d9fe8-5a2d-55ae-9ce6-47bd81a95ce0" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370019 1081 "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{289}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370019 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370019 1084 #uuid "e02416ae-0c05-5603-a201-57564dfca3b9" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370020 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370021 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370020 1069 "q-279154" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370020 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370020 1071 "![](images/92/9291f02901a4a05150dfadd309cc7508209c0f249d9b07a8185d1259f67d7296.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370020 1072 #uuid "eb865dca-23c4-59c4-bd03-8c9d8005b669" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370020 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{5}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{25}{4})}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\frac{49}{4})} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{49} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370021 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370022 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370021 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370021 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370021 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370022 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370021 1080 #uuid "bc0c2ace-3d6d-5114-b445-c9cf46779b74" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370022 1081 "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{49}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370022 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370022 1084 #uuid "983ea1d4-591d-51d0-91a0-baacaa311a91" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370023 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370024 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370023 1069 "q-279155" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370023 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370023 1071 "![](images/9d/9deebb67265aafd4fd2c3e7451dddc837c928242ac016a0632907816defcdec1.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?\n\n{{field-1}}$=1$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370023 1072 #uuid "11b50d3f-8a2a-53bd-9460-027070f70e56" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370023 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=\\frac{7}{2}$ and the vertical radius has length $b=\\frac{11}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{(\\frac{11}{2})}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(\\frac{49}{4})}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{(\\frac{121}{4})} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{121} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370024 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370025 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370024 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370024 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370024 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370025 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370024 1080 #uuid "357e7c5e-4b53-50fa-852d-a2a0c73a078a" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370025 1081 "\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{49}+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{121}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370025 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370025 1084 #uuid "9d1f7bc7-0cdd-5b27-8fe9-02b806603eb7" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370026 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370027 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370026 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370029 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370026 1069 "q-279198" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370026 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370026 1071 "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{24}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370026 1072 #uuid "2a5d8fed-b754-51cf-933f-f6f47cc1ecb4" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370026 1074 "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=8$ and ${b}^{2}=24.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=8,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{8}=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=24,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{24}=2\\sqrt{6}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $b=2\\sqrt{6},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{6}=4\\sqrt{6},$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}=4\\sqrt{2}.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370027 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370028 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370027 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370027 1077 "field-2" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370027 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370028 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370027 1080 #uuid "1784709b-8c12-545a-96ba-dc167b20f7c0" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370028 1081 "4\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370028 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370028 1084 #uuid "f0b4d7d8-6b13-51b3-8c16-f1f796b0790a" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370029 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370030 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370029 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370029 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370029 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370030 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370029 1080 #uuid "9c229291-398d-5b8b-8d76-841b2f6ef86c" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370030 1081 "4\\sqrt{6}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370030 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370030 1084 #uuid "e70a177c-e321-5d97-ad0f-2390d9f8f894" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370031 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370032 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370031 1069 "q-315980" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370031 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370031 1071 "![](images/e1/e13f4de3d3d66ba1205b3da8cbe8abec300a94d2e53453af3c9c12667e1d7907.png)\n\nA spherical soap bubble is expanding, causing its enclosed volume to increase at a rate of $24\\pi \\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s}.$ How fast is the radius increasing at the moment when the radius is $3\\text{ }\\text{cm}?$ Do not round the answer.\n\nThe radius of the bubble is increasing at a rate of \n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370031 1072 #uuid "958d1860-b4bf-5089-b184-17b91b607387" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370031 1074 "Let $r$ be the radius of the bubble and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=24\\pi \\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}$ when $r=3\\,\\text{cm}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the formula for the volume of a sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=24\\pi $ and $r=3,$ and solve for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi (3)^{2} \\\\ 24\\pi  & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 36\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when the radius is of the bubble is $3\\,\\text{cm},$ the radius is increasing at a rate of  $\\frac{2}{3}$ $\\text{ }\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s}.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370032 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370033 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370032 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370032 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370032 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370033 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370032 1080 #uuid "7c980452-fef1-5508-a37b-411c68c11df2" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370033 1081 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370033 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370033 1084 #uuid "8d8e4bc9-3a66-53c9-a3fb-31d9245a39e6" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370034 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370035 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370034 1069 "q-321979" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370034 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370034 1071 "According to the rational root theorem, the polynomial $f(x)=-{x}^{4}+2{x}^{3}-7x+30$ has{{field-1}} *potential* rational roots." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370034 1072 #uuid "30c939ec-fbca-5126-a442-cf703484d532" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370034 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $30,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,\\pm 5,\\pm 6,\\pm 10,\\pm 15,\\pm 30.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $-1,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{2}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{3}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{5}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{6}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{10}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{15}{1},\\text{ }\\pm \\frac{30}{1}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are only $\\pm 1,$ these simplify to:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\text{ }\\pm 2,\\text{ }\\pm 3,\\text{ }\\pm 5,\\text{ }\\pm 6,\\text{ }\\pm 10,\\text{ }\\pm 15,\\text{ }\\pm 30.\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. \n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370035 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370036 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370035 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370035 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370035 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370036 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370035 1080 #uuid "72a7d874-45df-5575-bf0d-420b6a82e0bb" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370036 1081 "16" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370036 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370036 1084 #uuid "d58f1e42-9acf-5bdd-844b-065909d75d35" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370037 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370038 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370037 1069 "q-32379" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370037 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370037 1071 "Which of the following vector-valued functions are continuous at $t=1?$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2{t}^{2},\\,|t-1|⟩ \\\\ g(t) & =⟨8{t}^{2}-2,\\,{2}^{t},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ h(t) & =⟨5t,\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+3),\\,7⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370037 1072 #uuid "b11c63d0-f1bb-5fe7-a785-a8527288e3bb" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370037 1074 "A vector-valued function is continuous at a point if and only if each of its components is continuous at that point.\n\nLet's consider the functions one-by-one. \n\n- The function $f(t)$ is continuous at $t=1$ because each of its components $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$, $2{t}^{2}$ and $|t-1|$ are continuous at $t=1.$\n\n- The function $g(t)$ is continuous at $t=1$ because each of its components $8{t}^{2}-2$, ${2}^{t}$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ are continuous at $t=1.$\n\n- The function $h(t)$ is continuous at $t=1$ because each of its components $5t$, $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t+3)$ and the constant function $7$ are continuous at $t=1.$\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ All the functions are continuous at $t=1$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370038 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370039 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370038 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370040 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370038 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370041 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370038 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370042 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370038 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370043 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370038 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370038 1077 "selection" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370038 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370040 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370038 1080 #uuid "b0bbed7a-7316-501c-a970-12ee74769825" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370039 1081 "None of the functions are continuous at $t=1$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370039 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370039 1084 #uuid "cdb8bcb9-0b4b-5345-ae43-a807513b5fa5" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370040 1081 "All the functions are continuous at $t=1$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370040 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370040 1084 #uuid "22bf18c0-2aa8-5919-a671-c699086ce1e2" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370041 1081 "$g(t)$ and $h(t)$ only" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370041 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370041 1084 #uuid "6ed44d1a-1194-54c2-93c2-d55971c2515b" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370042 1081 "$g(t)$ only" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370042 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370042 1084 #uuid "51450c28-70c1-59b4-bfd9-34ed393b7c96" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370043 1081 "$f(t)$ only" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370043 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370043 1084 #uuid "6867a8f4-5e59-5b99-a727-c1b4404e719a" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370044 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370045 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370044 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370047 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370044 1069 "q-323839" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370044 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370044 1071 "The polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}-16{x}^{2}+28x-16$ has a root in the interval $(1,\\frac{5}{2}).$ In **ascending order**, the **distinct** real roots of $f(x)$ are\n\n$x=$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,x=$\n {{field-2}}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370044 1072 #uuid "3523f32f-3ccc-5d04-8b63-ecf82061762c" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370044 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-16$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-16}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 8,\\,\\pm 16,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{2}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{4}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{8}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{16}{3}.\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(1,\\frac{5}{2})$ are $\\frac{4}{3}$ and $2.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x-\\frac{4}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\frac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $-16$ | $28$ | $-16$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $4$ | $-16$ | $16$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-12$ | $12$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x-\\frac{4}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x-\\frac{4}{3})(3{x}^{2}-12x+12).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-\\frac{4}{3})(3{x}^{2}-12x+12) \\\\  & =3(x-\\frac{4}{3})({x}^{2}-4x+4) \\\\  & =(3x-4){(x-2)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find that, in ascending order, the roots of the polynomial are $x=\\frac{4}{3}$  and $x=2.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370045 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370046 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370045 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370045 1077 "field-2" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370045 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370046 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370045 1080 #uuid "9709abec-d064-59aa-8c22-4afc48390779" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370046 1081 "2" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370046 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370046 1084 #uuid "e6fd439e-981c-51a0-a56f-c5a3c30c16ec" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370047 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370048 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370047 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370047 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370047 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370048 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370047 1080 #uuid "7f1d1400-785f-5d84-889