585 true] [17592186370064 1071 "Which of the following vector functions are continuous on $[1,4]?$\n\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(s) & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{s}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡s}\\,j+(s+1)\\,k \\\\ q(s) & =s{e}^{s}\\,i+{s}^{2}{e}^{s}\\,j-\\frac{s}{{e}^{s}}\\,k \\\\ r(s) & =\\sqrt{s}\\,i+(2-{s}^{2})\\,j+s\\operatorname{ln}⁡s\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370064 1072 #uuid "d8a80905-f66b-5034-9d8a-547a8fd2d157" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370064 1074 "A vector-valued function is continuous on an interval if and only if each of its components is continuous on that interval.  \n\nLet's consider the functions one-by-one. \n\n- The function  $p(s)$ is not continuous on $[1,4]$ because \nits second component, $\\frac{s}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡s}$ , is undefined at $s=1$ and hence discontinuous on $[1,4].$\n\n- The function $q(s)$ is continuous on $[1,4]$ because each of its components $s{e}^{s}$, ${s}^{2}{e}^{s}$ and $-\\frac{s}{{e}^{s}}$ are continuous on $[1,4].$\n\n- The function $r(s)$ is continuous on $[1,4]$ because each of its components $\\sqrt{s}$, $2-{s}^{2}$ and $s\\operatorname{ln}⁡s$ are continuous on $[1,4].$\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\n$\\,$ $q(s)$ and $r(s)$ only" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370065 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370066 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370065 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370067 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370065 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370068 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370065 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370069 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370065 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370070 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370065 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370065 1077 "selection" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370065 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370070 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370065 1080 #uuid "b84745d1-046d-5c26-be60-88a47c0e971f" 1319