49 17592186045585 true] [17592186370078 1072 #uuid "c2ed2ec8-f0b3-540a-a663-c1c0c0f9f6b1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370078 1074 "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=3+4{e}^{-x}.$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{-3x},\\,{y}_{2}={e}^{-x},\\,{y}_{3}={e}^{6x}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-3x} & {e}^{-x} & {e}^{6x} \\\\ -3{e}^{-3x} & -{e}^{-x} & 6{e}^{6x} \\\\ 9{e}^{-3x} & {e}^{-x} & 36{e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =({e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{-x}\\cdot {e}^{6x})|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 6 \\\\ 9 & 1 & 36\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[1(-36-6)-1(-108-54)+1(-3+9)] \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}(-42+162+6) \\\\  & =126{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{1},$ we compute the determinant\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x) & ={W}_{1}({y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x} & {e}^{6x} \\\\ -{e}^{-x} & 6{e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =6