d-4}} |  |  |  |\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is\n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370246 1072 #uuid "86b9d203-6c29-5757-800a-746bf5e7cb63" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370246 1074 "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=0.7x-0.35xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-0.4y+2xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(0.7x-0.35xy)=0.7-0.35y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(0.7x-0.35xy)=-0.35x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-0.4y+2xy)=2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-0.4y+2xy)=-0.4+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}0.7-0.35y & -0.35x \\\\ 2y & -0.4+2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}0.7-0.35(2) & -0.35(0.2) \\\\ 2(2) & -0.4+2(0.2)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -0.07 \\\\ 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-\\lambda  & -0.07 \\\\ 4 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-4\\cdot (-0.07) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.28 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=\\pm 0.2\\sqrt{7}\\,\\text{i}.$ Since the eigenvalues are purely imaginary, our equilibrium is $\\text{a stable}$ $\\text{center}.$\n\n**Note:**\nFor the Lotka-Volterra model (as in this problem), it is safe to classify the nonzero equilibrium as a center when the linearization has purely imaginary eigenvalues. \n\n**Watch out!** \nIn contrast, for a general nonlinear system, the Jacobian test alone is inconclusive (additional analysis is required to determine whether the equilibrium is a true center or a spiral sink/source)." 