n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}=x(1-x)-xy \\\\ {y}^{′}=-y+2xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the Lotka-Volterra model with carrying capacity above, where $x=x(t)$ represents the prey and $y=y(t)$ represents the predators. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.$\\,$ Evaluating the Jacobian of the system at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[0.5,\\,0.5]^{T},$ we get\n\n| $J({x}^{∗})=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |\n\n$\\,$ The eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are \n{{field-5}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the equilibrium at ${x}^{∗}$ is \n{{field-6}}{{field-7}}." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370546 1072 #uuid "b703c910-f4c3-56e2-961c-521faa3594f9" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370546 1074 "Let's denote ${f}_{1}(x,y)=x(1-x)-xy$ and ${f}_{2}(x,y)=-y+2xy.$\n\nNow, we compute the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x(1-x)-xy)=1-2x-y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x(1-x)-xy)=-x \\\\ \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-y+2xy)=2y,\\, & \\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-y+2xy)=-1+2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the Jacobian matrix is\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}1-2x-y & -x \\\\ 2y & -1+2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNext, we evaluate the Jacobian at the equilibrium ${x}^{∗}=[\\begin{aligned}0.5 \\\\ 0.5\\end{aligned}]$ to get\n\n$$\nJ({x}^{∗})=[\\begin{aligned}1-2(0.5)-0.5 & -0.5 \\\\ 2(0.5) & -1+2(0.5)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-0.5 & -0.5 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix, we write down the characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-0.5-\\lambda  & -0.5 \\\\ 1 & -\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-0.5-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-1(-0.5) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (0.5+\\lambda )+0.5 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+0.5\\lambda +0.5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation, we get the eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1,2}=-0.25\\pm 0.25\\sq