5585 true] [17592186370581 1084 #uuid "cecbaacb-b338-5572-8718-1c1887a2eb55" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370582 1081 "4" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370582 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370582 1084 #uuid "623041d2-5b8d-5fef-af5c-a67b53157ed8" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370583 1081 "0" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370583 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370583 1084 #uuid "3b120c2e-f922-5c0b-be29-bdf619e49d94" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370584 1081 "-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370584 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370584 1084 #uuid "2f5afbb1-0eca-524c-913c-15b73891c04e" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370585 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370586 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370585 1069 "q-337347" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370585 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370585 1071 "At a carnival, a ring toss game costs $$2$ per play. If the player successfully lands a ring, they win $$30.$ Otherwise, the player wins nothing. Suppose the probability of winning on any single play is $\\frac{1}{20}.$ What is the expected profit, rounded to the nearest dollar, for a player who plays forty games in a row?\n\nThe expected profit in dollars is $E(p)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370585 1072 #uuid "86be754d-56ad-514c-93c6-7c5f0dfd92c3" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370585 1074 "First of all, profit is revenue minus cost, or in our case, winnings minus cost. So, once we have found the expected winnings after forty games, we need to subtract $40\\cdot 2=80$ dollars to account for the price of playing.\n\nNow, let's find the expected winnings and summarize them in a table. If $W$ is the winnings (in dollars) on a single game, and $f(w)$ is the probability mass function for $W,$ then we have the following:\n\n| $f(w)$ | $\\frac{1}{20}$ | $\\frac{19}{20}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $w$ | $30$ | $0$ |\n\nThe expected value of $W$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =30\\cdot f(30)+0\\cdot f(0) \\\\  & =30(\\frac{1}{20})+0(\\frac{19}{20}) \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis represents the expected winnings for a single game. So, the expected winnings for $40$ games is\n\n$$\n40\\cdot \\text{E}[W]=40\\cdot 1.5=60.\n$$\n\nNow, subtracting the $80$ dollars paid to play, we get that the expected profit (in dollars) is\n\n$$\nE[p]=60-80=-20.\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370586 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370587 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370586 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370586 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370586 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370587 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370586 1080 #uuid "74681756-dc23-5c60-a37f-5d97898c8abb" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370587 1081 "-20" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370587 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370587 1084 #uuid "9b2e18a4-b208-5a81-8c65-7e48082fe24d" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370588 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370589 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370588 1069 "q-344668" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370588 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370588 1071 "The radius of a circle is increasing. At a particular moment, the rate of increase of the area of the circle is numerically equal to one-fourth of the rate of increase of the diameter. What is the radius $r$ of the circle at that moment? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370588 1072 #uuid "9cb3766f-2b46-5511-a3aa-af946fffbb79" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370588 1074 "Let $r$ be the radius, $A$ the area, and $D$ the diameter of the circle. We wish to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nTo start, let's set up an equation relating the area $A$ and the diameter $D$ of the circle. Since $D=2r$ and $A=\\pi {r}^{2},$ the relationship between $A$ and $D$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}=\\pi {(\\frac{D}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}D}(\\frac{\\pi {D}^{2}}{4}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}D}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4} & =\\frac{\\pi D}{2} \\\\ D & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, since $D=2r,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2r & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{4\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370589 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370590 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370589 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370589 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370589 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370590 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370589 1080 #uuid "f21a8153-dd04-5ca4-9eb0-c061710ecf2b" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370590 1081 "\\frac{1}{4\\pi }" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370590 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370590 1084 #uuid "f462d92e-67a0-5450-8306-049e053bbaa4" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370591 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370592 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370591 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370593 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370591 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370594 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370591 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370595 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370591 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370596 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370591 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370591 1077 "selection" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370591 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370595 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370591 1080 #uuid "df241155-a0fa-5861-ae68-a9bddf99f329" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370592 1081 "-2x" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370592 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370592 1084 #uuid "91c5438f-6132-5a3e-b472-d10ac6b0dfc0" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370593 1081 "10x" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370593 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370593 1084 #uuid "9540fce5-e3f3-50d3-910a-dc561ace6926" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370594 1081 "-4{x}^{2}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370594 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370594 1084 #uuid "85c8902a-e973-512e-b592-e0112e464bcc" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370595 1081 "{x}^{2}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370595 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370595 1084 #uuid "8214c7a6-6d34-5b0b-8c0b-df817c9359ac" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370596 1081 "5{x}^{2}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370596 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370596 1084 #uuid "850643e6-48d1-5e18-b823-fe281530bf4d" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370597 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370598 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370597 1069 "q-344748" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370597 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370597 1071 "![](images/5b/5bf71c5c613c69263e15137bbd5aa56716a8c289a4457a95a9c7b1fddd8af03b.png)\n\nA spherical balloon is inflated at the rate of $18{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$ If at a particular moment the balloon's radius is changing at the rate $\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\text{ cm/s},$ then what is the radius of the balloon at this moment? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{cm}$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370597 1072 #uuid "bd0df49c-cf08-5e74-9577-2fdce0a0be4e" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370597 1074 "Let $r$ be the radius of the balloon and $V$ its volume. Both $r$ and $V$ change with time. In particular, we are given\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=18{\\text{ cm}}^{3}\\text{/s},\n$$\n\nand we want to find $r$ when $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\text{ cm/s}.$\n\nTo start, let's write down the volume formula for the sphere and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(V) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\frac{4}{3}\\pi {r}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=18$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{2\\pi },$ and solve for $r.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18 & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }\\cdot 4\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 18 & =2{r}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\ r & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius at that moment is $3\\text{ cm}.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370598 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370599 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370598 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370598 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370598 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370599 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370598 1080 #uuid "dee1c9b6-29b9-55de-b317-a70304dccc69" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370599 1081 "3" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370599 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370599 1084 #uuid "449bf8c9-d854-535b-9472-7eb27966c6e5" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370600 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370601 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370600 1069 "q-344789" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370600 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370600 1071 "![](images/07/07717562b991e4b2907db5bcd51ccea451e788d4490dc6111225077dee018bde.png)\n\nThe area of a circular oil spill starts from zero and grows at a constant rate of $12\\pi {\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ Determine the length of the radius at the moment when it is increasing at a rate of $3\\text{ m/s}.$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m}$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370600 1072 #uuid "fe129eff-1d93-5824-989a-6869b87595aa" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370600 1074 "In this situation, we want to find $r$ at the moment when $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3,$ and we are also told that  $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=12\\pi .$\n\nTo start, let's write down the area formula for the circle and then differentiate it using implicit differentiation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we can substitute the given information $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=12\\pi $ and $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=3,$ and solve for $r.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12\\pi  & =3\\cdot 2\\pi r \\\\ 12\\pi  & =6\\pi r \\\\ r & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the radius at that moment is $2\\text{ m}.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370601 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370602 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370601 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370601 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370601 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370602 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370601 1080 #uuid "7d3260f1-106a-545d-8b2a-201833242034" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370602 1081 "2" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370602 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370602 1084 #uuid "20d1197f-5e03-5fe5-9568-54351f5a3411" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370603 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370604 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370603 1069 "q-344792" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370603 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370603 1071 "$$\n{y}^{‴}-2{y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}-8y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\,{{y}_{1}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}\n$$\n\nConsider the differential equation and the set of fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{2x}+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+C\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+{u}_{1}(x){e}^{2x}+{u}_{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+{u}_{3}(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{3}(x)?$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370603 1072 #uuid "b419d437-3157-5dfe-877b-fe449ea99015" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370603 1074 "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{2x},\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 4{e}^{2x} & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2 & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x) & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 4 & -4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[(8{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+8{cos}^{2}⁡(2x))-2(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x))+4(2{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)+2{cos}^{2}⁡(2x))] \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}[8-2(0)+4(2)] \\\\  & =16{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{3},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{3}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{3}(x) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)-2{e}^{2x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2x}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{3}(x) & =(-1)^{3-3}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{3}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))(-2{e}^{2x})(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x))}{16{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\int ({cos}^{2}⁡(2x)-{sin}^{2}⁡(2x))\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370605 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370606 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370607 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370608 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370604 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370609 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370604 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370604 1077 "selection" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370604 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370607 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370604 1080 #uuid "002d2e51-519d-543b-bc7f-e80d5edae8b7" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370605 1081 "\\frac{1}{32}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370605 1083 #edb/ref 1759218604