 fundamental solutions of the corresponding homogeneous equation above. \nUsing the method of variation of parameters, the general solution of the equation can be written in the form\n\n$$\ny(x)=A{e}^{x}+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{u}_{1}(x){e}^{x}+{u}_{2}(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+{u}_{3}(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWhat is the function ${u}_{2}(x)?$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370617 1072 #uuid "a6847fd7-278a-5502-98f5-ed3a61399d2f" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370617 1074 "According to the method of variation of parameters, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}={u}_{1}{y}_{1}+{u}_{2}{y}_{2}+{u}_{3}{y}_{3},\\,{u}_{i}=(-1)^{3-i}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{i}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,i=1,2,3,\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)$ is the forcing term, $W(x)$ is the Wronskian of ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{1}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{2}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}|,\\,{W}_{3}(x)=|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{2}^{′}\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, the forcing term is $g(x)=-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).$ Additionally, we're given the fundamental solutions\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,{y}_{3}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's verify that the fundamental solutions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are independent by computing the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(x) & =W({y}_{1},{y}_{2},{y}_{3}) \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {e}^{x} & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {e}^{x} & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 1 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ 1 & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x}[1({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-1(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+1({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)] \\\\  & ={e}^{x}[1(1)-1(0)+1(1)] \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the Wronskian is not equal to zero, this confirms that the functions ${y}_{1},$ ${y}_{2},$ and ${y}_{3}$ are linearly independent. \n\nNow, to find ${u}_{2},$ we compute the determinant ${W}_{2}(x)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{W}_{2}(x) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{′} & {y}_{3}^{′}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x}\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}1 & \\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & ={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{2}(x) & =(-1)^{3-2}\\int \\frac{g(x){W}_{2}(x)}{W(x)}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\int \\frac{-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot {e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{2{e}^{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x-{sin}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370618 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370619 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370618 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370620 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370618 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370621 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370618 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370622 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370618 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370623 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370618 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [