limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-4}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{-4}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[-{u}^{-1}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{a}^{-4} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{1}{(-4)}-\\frac{1}{a}] \\\\  & =-(-\\frac{1}{4}-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370640 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370641 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370640 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370640 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370640 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370641 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370640 1080 #uuid "4c1b6083-72a1-5e77-9dc4-4a2062b1b215" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370641 1081 "\\frac{1}{4}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370641 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370641 1084 #uuid "341312de-df8d-50a0-8b5d-89acf6c49904" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370642 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370643 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370642 1069 "q-351014" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370642 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370642 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=6{t}^{2}-12{t}^{5}+4,$ where $t$ is the time in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=3\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=2\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370642 1072 #uuid "94778af5-659c-5c62-adde-ae157e048fcd" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370642 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (6{t}^{2}-12{t}^{5}+4)\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{t}^{3}-2{t}^{6}+4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=3.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & =2({0}^{3})-2({0}^{6})+4(0)+C \\\\ 3 & =0+C \\\\ C & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=2{t}^{3}-2{t}^{6}+4t+3.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=2\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =2({2}^{3})-2({2}^{6})+4(2)+3 \\\\  & =2(8)-2(64)+8+3 \\\\  & =16-128+8+3 \\\\  & =-101\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $101\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370643 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370644 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370643 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370643 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370643 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370644 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370643 1080 #uuid "8cfdb290-6d6a-5b1d-8562-d1ee72096ea6" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370644 1081 "101" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370644 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370644 1084 #uuid "2aa1c1b1-5b14-5c85-9d8c-fd56b3f6b3c3" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370645 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370646 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370645 1069 "q-351050" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370645 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370645 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration, measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ given by the function $a(t)=\\frac{1}{t}+4,$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. Find the velocity $v(t),$ in $\\text{m/s},$ of the particle at time $t$ given that the particle has velocity $v=6\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=1\\,\\text{s}.$\n\n$v(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370645 1072 #uuid "5b09d8b5-38a6-571f-97fc-401ea277cbf9" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370645 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{t}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{t}\\,\\text{d}t+\\int 4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(t)+4t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(1)=6.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+4(1)+C \\\\ 6 & =4+C \\\\ C & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+4t+2.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370646 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370647 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370646 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370646 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370646 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370647 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370646 1080 #uuid "8984843f-be02-5c0f-8efd-482db5e691eb" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370647 1081 "\\operatorname{ln}(t)+4t+2" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370647 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370647 1084 #uuid "69a099bb-cf37-50ce-87c7-dffcefbebe10" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370648 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370649 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370648 1069 "q-351056" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370648 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370648 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370648 1072 #uuid "265ea233-2d78-572b-bc82-4120830c1cf7" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370648 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =-2+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{5}{2}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{5}{2}-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}-2(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{3}{2}\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370649 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370650 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370649 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370649 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370649 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370650 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370649 1080 #uuid "7218f237-1d89-5050-a62e-4ced6034fc1f" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370650 1081 "\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370650 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370650 1084 #uuid "09b57713-d778-515f-b755-ad9841384591" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370651 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370652 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370651 1069 "q-351057" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370651 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370651 1071 "A particle moves along a straight line relative to a fixed origin $O$ with acceleration $a(t)=4{sec}^{2}⁡t,$ where $a(t)$ is measured in ${\\text{ft/s}}^{2}$ and $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the speed of the particle at the moment $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}$ given that the particle has velocity $v=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}$ when $t=0\\,\\text{s}.$\n\nThe speed at the moment $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}$ is\n{{field-1}} $\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370651 1072 #uuid "1d3fa995-ddc0-5a6e-8d7e-dd92d574e919" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370651 1074 "First, we find $v(t)$ by integrating the acceleration with respect to time:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int 4{sec}^{2}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C$, we use the fact that $v(0)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =4\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =0+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the velocity $v$ of the particle at time $t$ is $v(t)=\\frac{1}{2}+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.$\n\nWe evaluate the velocity at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{1}{2}+4\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+4(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the speed is equal to $|v(t)|,$ we conclude that the speed of the particle is $\\frac{9}{2}\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370652 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370653 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370652 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370652 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370652 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370653 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370652 1080 #uuid "1e09cad6-b131-5404-84b5-5bb689714c45" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370653 1081 "\\frac{9}{2}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370653 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370653 1084 #uuid "f3ed7280-25ef-5c88-b765-e9d4e0a66aeb" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370654 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370655 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370654 1069 "q-351848" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370654 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370654 1071 "The fifth-degree Taylor polynomial of $f(x)$ about $x=-1$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{5}(x)=3+7(x+1)-2(x+1)^{2}+5(x+1)^{3}+\\frac{11}{24}(x+1)^{4}-\\frac{2}{15}(x+1)^{5}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${f}^{(4)}(-1)?$\n\n${f}^{(4)}(-1)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370654 1072 #uuid "2d3ee76d-cfc0-53dd-83af-b34a8a691671" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370654 1074 "The coefficient of $(x+1)^{4}$ is given by $\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(-1)}{4!}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{f}^{(4)}(-1)}{4!} & =\\frac{11}{24} \\\\ \\frac{{f}^{(4)}(-1)}{24} & =\\frac{11}{24} \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(-1) & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370655 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370656 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370655 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370655 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370655 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370656 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370655 1080 #uuid "36287c92-a798-5219-9697-9f711e4caf63" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370656 1081 "11" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370656 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370656 1084 #uuid "05de5e98-c241-5881-a6ee-094e13acd299" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370657 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370658 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370657 1069 "q-353407" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370657 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370657 1071 "Consider the arc of the curve $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}})$ that lies between the lines $y=0$ and $y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$ Calculate the surface area $S$ generated when this arc is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$S=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370657 1072 #uuid "f03e8c84-5169-5dac-b364-f442e668849d" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370657 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $x=f(y),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${y}_{1}=0$ to ${y}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{1-{y}^{2}}))=\\frac{2y}{1-{y}^{2}}\\,⇒\\,{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}=\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{(1-{y}^{2})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{y}_{1}}^{{y}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}y})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{(1-{y}^{2})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{2}}y\\sqrt{\\frac{(1+{y}^{2})^{2}}{(1-{y}^{2})^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{2}}y\\cdot \\frac{1+{y}^{2}}{1-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the substitution \n\n$$\nu=1-{y}^{2}\\,⇒\\,-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{d}u=y\\,\\text{d}y,\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1/\\sqrt{2}}y\\cdot \\frac{1+{y}^{2}}{1-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{1/2}\\frac{2-u}{u}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{1/2}^{1}(\\frac{2}{u}-1)\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\pi (2\\operatorname{ln}⁡u-u)\\,{|}_{1/2}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi ((0-1)-(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})-\\frac{1}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2-1)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370658 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370659 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370658 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370658 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370658 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370659 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370658 1080 #uuid "773e8a61-3cb2-5797-a423-210c87c3339b" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370659 1081 "\\frac{\\pi (4\\operatorname{ln}2-1)}{2}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370659 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370659 1084 #uuid "8baeb63a-e580-579b-b44a-2f3a0e589a14" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370661 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370666 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370671 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370675 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370660 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370680 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370660 1068 #edb/ref 17