\n\nSince the two linearly independent solutions are already mutually orthogonal, we only need to normalize them. Hence, we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$\n\n| | ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-7}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ |  |  |  | | | ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-8}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-9}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $1$ |  |  |  | |  |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-7}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $0$ |  |  |  |\n| ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-8}} |  |  |\n|  | {{field-9}} |  |  |  |\n|  | $1$ |  |  |  |" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370660 1072 #uuid "d4516c67-259f-5935-b3cd-af66ef9e5af8" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370660 1074 "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=5\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{10} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}{{[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370661 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370662 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370661 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370663 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370661 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370664 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370661 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370665 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370661 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370661 1077 "field-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370661 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370664 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370661 1080 #uuid "c2867685-0421-53b7-b86e-8ca6767a7db9" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370662 1081 "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" 13194139533449 175921860455