\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,-{x}_{2}+2{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{1}{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{1}}+{x}_{3}{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables.\n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we only need to normalize them. This gives the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370705 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370706 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370707 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370704 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370708 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370704 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045494 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370704 1077 "field-6" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370704 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370708 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370704 1080 #uuid "8625826a-cc23-5758-bebd-017366704bfa" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370705 1081 "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370705 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370705 1084 #uuid "1f2f233f-38be-5891-a35e-c9b9264936bb" 1319