 "7c7a5ad0-60ed-5522-a987-5398faa11e7a" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370852 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370853 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370852 1069 "q-365881" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370852 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370852 1071 "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨y,\\,z,\\,2x-y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined in Cartesian coordinates as $z=3$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S$ with respect to the *downward*-pointing unit normal vector $n.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370852 1072 #uuid "41a56a15-fd44-52b0-9c06-c58863771399" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370852 1074 "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is a continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor the surface $z=3$ for  $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1,$ the downward-pointing unit normal vector is\n\n$$\nn=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨y,\\,z,\\,2x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,3,\\,2x-y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-(2x-y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}(-(2x-y))\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-(2x-y))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{2}(-(2x-y))\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}[({x}^{2}-xy){|}_{x=1}^{x=2}\\,]\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3-y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[3y-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370853 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370854 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370853 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370855 131