1069 "q-38819" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370886 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370886 1071 "A plate of mass $M$ occupies the region $D$ bounded by the curve $y=\\sqrt{x},$ the line $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. Its mass density at any point on the plate is given by $\\lambda (x,y)=4My.$ Find the radius of gyration of the plate about the $x$-axis." 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370886 1072 #uuid "defce080-8c65-59c1-a4eb-652286fe6d3f" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370886 1074 "For a plane lamina in the shape of a region $D$ with mass $M$ and mass density function $\\lambda (x,y),$ we have the following definitions:\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{x}$ about the $x$-axis is given by\n${R}_{x}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{x}$ is the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{y}$ about the $y$-axis is given by\n${R}_{y}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{y}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{y}$ is the moment of inertia about the $y$-axis.\n\n- The radius of gyration ${R}_{0}$ about the origin is given by\n${R}_{0}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{0}}{M}},$\nwhere ${I}_{0}$ is the moment of inertia about the origin.\n\nThe moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is defined as\n\n$$\n{I}_{x}={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\lambda (x,y)$ is the mass density function. The region $D$ is a type I region with representation\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}}.\n$$\n\nHence, the moment of inertia about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}_{x} & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{2}\\lambda (x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}{y}^{2}\\cdot 4My\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4M{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =M{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{M}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the radius of gyration about the $x$-axis is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{I}_{x}}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{M}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{M}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370888 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370889 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370890 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370891 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370887 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370892 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370887 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370887 1077 "selection" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370887 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370892 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370887 1080 #uuid "4a96fbcd-7761-58d9-ae0a-eb2b94c9e2c7" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370888 1081 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370888 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370888 1084 #uuid "f293b5e9-65d5-536c-b18d-a76dc4e7ea80" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370889 1081 "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370889 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370889 1084 #uuid "2e26a64f-d68e-5522-ac57-78a01cfc8093" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370890 1081 "\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{5}}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370890 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370890 1084 #uuid "de7b89dc-f977-5e1a-9b52-09259a14b526" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370891 1081 "1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370891 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370891 1084 #uuid "6b112ea6-1937-5361-af39-bf68f7bc8dd2" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370892 1081 "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370892 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370892 1084 #uuid "696ba733-0b46-5f03-b9f3-48ae1fe194cf" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370893 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370894 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370893 1069 "q-39368" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370893 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370893 1071 "Find the scale factor of the transformation represented by the rotation-scaling matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & -2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3} & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}].$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370893 1072 #uuid "37b80ee6-abc6-539a-a182-cf1dfa9d90ec" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370893 1074 "The scale factor $r$ of the rotation-scaling matrix $[\\begin{aligned}a & -b \\\\ b & a\\end{aligned}]$ can be found using the formula \n\n$$\nr=\\sqrt{det(A)}=\\sqrt{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{det(A)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+12} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{49}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370894 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370895 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370894 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370896 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370894 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370897 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370894 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370898 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370894 1075 #edb/ref 17592186370899 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370894 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370894 1077 "selection" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370894 1079 #edb/ref 17592186370895 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370894 1080 #uuid "b0af04e7-8b4b-55f4-b694-abe80ab362c6" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370895 1081 "\\frac{7}{2}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370895 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370895 1084 #uuid "e67c093a-7510-53fd-95c3-b7d2aa9e8a4a" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370896 1081 "3" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370896 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370896 1084 #uuid "b30f02c2-408a-58bd-87a3-f936d828b357" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370897 1081 "2" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370897 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370897 1084 #uuid "a38ecd8e-e92d-54d1-a4b3-ae5e029cdefa" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370898 1081 "\\sqrt{\\frac{5}{3}}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370898 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370898 1084 #uuid "0a96b567-a806-58be-a27c-428256a56aad" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370899 1081 "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370899 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370899 1084 #uuid "57dff8a3-cee2-55d6-929e-d7e89292062d" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370900 1068 #edb/ref 17592186370901 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370900 1069 "q-39477" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370900 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370900 1071 "Consider the orthogonal set $U$ below. By adding a new vector $x=[{x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3}]^{T},$ extend the set so that it forms an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370900 1072 #uuid "75f96dd3-b802-5102-880f-b4c303f55070" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370900 1074 "Let's denote the vectors of $U$ as ${u}_{1},\\,{u}_{2}.$ To extend the given set to an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$, we need to find a third vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]$ such that\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}+7{x}_{3}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the augmented matrix $M$ of the system above and reduce it to its row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -4 & 7 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{3}{5}){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\frac{26}{5} & \\frac{38}{5} & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns ($1$st and $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we get ${x}_{2}=\\frac{19}{13}{x}_{3}.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1} & ={x}_{3}-2(\\frac{19}{13}{x}_{3}) \\\\ 5{x}_{1} & =-\\frac{25}{13}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{1} & =-\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{13}{x}_{3} \\\\ \\frac{19}{13}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{3}=13$ we obtain the vector \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 19 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthogonal basis of ${R}^{3}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 19 \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{x}_{1}}{{x}_{2}}=-\\frac{5}{19}.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186370901 1075 #edb/ref 17592186