3194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371041 1072 #uuid "bced920a-390c-58ad-9284-d7bd9d3f7981" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371041 1074 "A vector-valued function is differentiable at a point if and only if each of its components is differentiable at that point. \n\nLet's consider the functions one-by-one. \n\n- The derivative of the second component $|t-1|$ of $f(t)$ is not defined at $t=1.$ So, we conclude that $f(t)$ is not differentiable at this point.\n\n- Next, we differentiate $g(t)$  to find ${g}^{′}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince each component of ${g}^{′}(t)$ is defined   at $t=1$, we conclude that $g(t)$ is differentiable at this point.\n\n- Finally, the  second component $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\pi t)$ of  $h(t)$ is not defined at $t=1.$ So, we conclude that $h(t)$ is not differentiable at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$g(t)$ only.\"" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371042 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371043 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371042 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371044 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371042 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371045 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371042 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371046 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371042 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371047 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371042 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371042 1077 "selection" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371042 1079 #edb/ref 17592186371045 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371042 1080 #uuid "16e6fdb0-87e9-5922-a9a7-5680bc0ffa48" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371043 1081 "$f(t)$ and $g(t)$ only" 13194139533449 17592186