olynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-4\\alpha +13(\\alpha x+\\beta )=39x+1\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n13\\alpha x+(13\\beta -4\\alpha )=39x+1.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}13\\alpha =39\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 13\\beta -4\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =3,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{2x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+3x+1.\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371119 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371120 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371119 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371121 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371119 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371122 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371119 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371123 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371119 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371124 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371119 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371119 1077 "selection" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371119 1079 #edb/ref 17592186371121 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371119 1080 #uuid "f595e04