basis of ${R}^{2}.$ What is the value of $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|?$\n\n$$\nU={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}]}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371125 1072 #uuid "1f896c29-487a-51e0-95fe-118554647621" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371125 1074 "Let's denote the vector of $U$ as ${u}_{1}.$ To extend the given set to an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$, we need to find a second vector $x$ such that $‖x‖=1,$ and\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot x=0\\,⟹\\,\\frac{3}{5}{x}_{1}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\nFrom the above equation, we get ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{2}.$\n\nSo, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{4}{3}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-3,$ we obtain the vector \n$x=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nNow, we need to normalize $x.$ Notice that $‖x‖=\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+(-3)^{2}}=5.$ So, we normalize $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{‖x‖}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, an orthonormal basis of ${R}^{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5} \\\\ \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $|{x}_{1}|+|{x}_{2}|=|\\frac{4}{5}|+|-\\frac{3}{5}|=\\frac{7}{5}.$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371127 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371128 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371129 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371130 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371126 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371131 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371126 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371126 1077 "selection" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371126 1079 #edb/ref 17592186371127 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371126 