t{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute the probability mass function $f(x).$ Here, there are $7$ possible outcomes ($1,2,3,4,5,6,7$) and each outcome has the same probability, so the probability mass function is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ | $6$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ | $\\frac{1}{7}$ |\n\nNext, we compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+2\\cdot f(2)+3\\cdot f(3)+4\\cdot f(4)+5\\cdot f(5)+6\\cdot f(6)+7\\cdot f(7) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{7})+2(\\frac{1}{7})+3(\\frac{1}{7})+4(\\frac{1}{7})+5(\\frac{1}{7})+6(\\frac{1}{7})+7(\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{2}^{2}\\cdot f(2)+{3}^{2}\\cdot f(3)+{4}^{2}\\cdot f(4)+{5}^{2}\\cdot f(5)+{6}^{2}\\cdot f(6)+{7}^{2}\\cdot f(7) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{1}{7})+4(\\frac{1}{7})+9(\\frac{1}{7})+16(\\frac{1}{7})+25(\\frac{1}{7})+36(\\frac{1}{7})+49(\\frac{1}{7}) \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =20-{(4)}^{2} \\\\  & =20-16 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{SD}[X] & =\\sqrt{\\text{Var}[X]} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371236 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371237 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371236 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371238 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371236 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371239 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371236 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371240 131941395