lementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+10y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3\\pm \\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +6(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+10(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=5{x}^{2}-4x+5\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n10\\alpha {x}^{2}+(12\\alpha +10\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +6\\beta +10\\gamma )=5{x}^{2}-4x+5\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}10\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 12\\alpha +10\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +6\\beta +10\\gamma =5\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =-1,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by \n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x+1.\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371257 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371258 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371257 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371259 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371257 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371260 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371257 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371261 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371257 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371262 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371257 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371257 1077 "selection" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371257 1079 #edb/ref 17592186371261 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371257 1080 #uuid "a7d23e8b-11b4-5ec1-a02e-b04b713d3e20" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371258 1081 "y={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+4" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371258 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371258 1084 #uuid "98457a6f-2eb2-5f65-b07b-9d0471a1ae7b" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371259 1081 "y={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2{x}^{2}-2x-1" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371259 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371259 1084 #uuid "22f7247c-bbec-57c3-a6cf-ab9990130c34" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371260 1081 "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{3x}-2{x}^{2}-x+4" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371260 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371260 1084 #u