n that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the value of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371296 1072 #uuid "fa5f61d9-1d7f-5da9-9527-e7cc3f414c94" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371296 1074 "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=10$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=4.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=10.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=100.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 7 \\\\ 7 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}58 & 42 \\\\ 42 & 58\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-100I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-100I=[\\begin{aligned}58 & 42 \\\\ 42 & 58\\end{aligned}]-100[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-42 & 42 \\\\ 42 & -42\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-100I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}|=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371297 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371298 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371297 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371299 13194139533449 175921860455