6465-4768-5a68-bff5-a66b32ecef99" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371329 1081 "I and II only" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371329 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371329 1084 #uuid "41e2d886-d9dd-5f3c-9d82-437bf3ca24fa" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371330 1081 "II only" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371330 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045500 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371330 1084 #uuid "d08a0f98-9272-54d2-8beb-7fd66302a9f5" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371331 1068 #edb/ref 17592186371332 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371331 1069 "q-96225" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371331 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371331 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}5\\sqrt{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ what is the second column of $U?$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371331 1072 #uuid "11c9ca5d-0538-529f-a538-b0377a5d764a" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371331 1074 "Recall that if $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ and ${\\sigma }_{i}\\ne 0,$ then the $i$th column of $U$ can be found as\n\n$$\n{u}_{i}=\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{i}}A{v}_{i},\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere ${u}_{i}$ and ${v}_{i}$ are the $i$th columns of the orthogonal matrices $U$ and $V,$ respectively.  \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=5\\sqrt{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$ Therefore, the first column of $U$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{15}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, we can't use the same formula to find the second column of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find a vector\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that \n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,‖{u}_{2}‖=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{1}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}{x}_{2}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get a nonzero solution $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ Normalizing this vector, we finally obtain the  second column of $U:$\n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371333 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371334 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371335 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371336 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371332 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371337 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371332 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371332 1077 "selection" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371332 1079 #edb/ref 17592186371335 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371332 1080 #uuid "8195b55c-1ddc-5554-bbb1-d1c6f2e9875c" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371333 1081 "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}]" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [1759218