lue of $|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|?$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371338 1072 #uuid "01aea047-32df-527d-acda-292ec1b804c9" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371338 1074 "If $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$ the columns of $V$ must be the right singular vectors of $A,$ i.e., the unit eigenvectors of the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A.$ \n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that the singular values are ${\\sigma }_{1}=7$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=3.$ So, the first column of $V$ corresponds to the singular value ${\\sigma }_{1}=7.$\n\nHence, we need to find a unit eigenvector of ${A}^{T}\\,A$ that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}=49.$ \n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A & ={[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}41 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Computing ${A}^{T}\\,A-49I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{T}\\,A-49I=[\\begin{aligned}41 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 17\\end{aligned}]-49[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -16 \\\\ -16 & -32\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $({A}^{T}\\,A-49I)v=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ Dividing $v$ by its norm, we get \n\n$$\n\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n|{v}_{11}|+|{v}_{21}|=|-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}|+|\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}|=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{5}}.\n$$" 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371339 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371340 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371339 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371341 13194139533449 17592186045585 true] [17592186371339 1075 #edb/ref 17592186371342 131941